{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 4,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.912,
            0.049,
            0.955,
            0.068
          ],
          "content": "157"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.08,
            0.727,
            0.19
          ],
          "content": "List the materials of the objects in Table 11.1 and **classify** them into transparent, translucent, and opaque (In the chapter ‘Materials Around Us’ in the Grade 6 Science textbook Curiosity you learnt to classify materials into transparent, translucent, and opaque, depending on how you could see through them)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.191,
            0.6,
            0.282
          ],
          "content": "Go to a dark room, turn on the torch, and place it at such a position that you get a spot of light from the torch on a wall. Or you may place a cardboard screen as shown in Fig. 11.6 and get the spot of light on it."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.283,
            0.6,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "We will now conduct this activity in two parts — prediction and observation."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.613,
            0.137,
            0.891,
            0.273
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.656,
                0.272,
                0.862,
                0.304
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 11.6: Passing light through different materials"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.321,
            0.728,
            0.393
          ],
          "content": "**Predict** what will happen if you hold an object in front of the light coming out of the torch. Would you continue to see the spot of light on the screen? Note your prediction in Table 11.1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.179,
            0.395,
            0.728,
            0.449
          ],
          "content": "Now, actually place the object between the torch and the screen. Does light pass through the object? Note your observation in Table 11.1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.451,
            0.451,
            0.47
          ],
          "content": "Repeat this for all the objects."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.472,
            0.728,
            0.562
          ],
          "content": "Was your observation the same as your prediction? What conclusions could you draw? Light passes almost completely through **transparent** materials. Light passes partially through **translucent** materials. Light does not pass through **opaque** materials."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.221,
            0.576,
            0.566,
            0.679
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.702,
            0.484,
            0.725
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "11.4 Shadow Formation"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.634,
            0.572,
            0.88,
            0.744
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.744,
            0.621,
            0.817
          ],
          "content": "What did you see on the screen in Activity 11.3 when an opaque object was placed in the path of light? Did you see a dark patch on the wall? Why was this dark patch formed?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.818,
            0.621,
            0.891
          ],
          "content": "We now know that light travels in a straight line. So, when an opaque object is placed in its path, light is blocked. The dark patch, where light does not reach, is the **shadow**."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.634,
            0.753,
            0.883,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
